::ALG2_U04_L08_GP_Q01::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L08_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: Unit 4 standards synthesis; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
Which model represents 1000 dollars growing by 5% annually?
{
=A. A(t)=1000(1.05)ᵗ#Correct. The initial value is 1000 and 5% growth gives factor 1.05.
~B. A(t)=1000(0.95)ᵗ#This models 5% decay.
~C. A(t)=1.05(1000)ᵗ#This reverses parameters.
~D. A(t)=1000+5t#This is linear.
}

::ALG2_U04_L08_GP_Q02::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L08_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: Unit 4 standards synthesis; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
What is log_2(64)?
{
~A. 4#2 to the fourth is 16.
=B. 6#Correct. 2 raised to the sixth power is 64.
~C. 8#2 to the eighth is 256.
~D. 32#This is not the needed exponent.
}

::ALG2_U04_L08_GP_Q03::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L08_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: Unit 4 standards synthesis; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
Expand log_3(xy²).
{
~A. log_3(x+y²)#This changes multiplication to addition inside the argument.
~B. 2log_3(xy)#The exponent applies only to y.
=C. log_3(x)+2log_3(y)#Correct. Use product and power properties.
~D. log_3(x)-2log_3(y)#Subtraction would represent division.
}

::ALG2_U04_L08_GP_Q04::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L08_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: Unit 4 standards synthesis; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
Solve log_5(x)=3.
{
~A. x=15#This multiplies base and output.
~B. x=8#This adds base and output.
~C. x=2#This subtracts incorrectly.
=D. x=125#Correct. Rewrite as x=5³.
}

::ALG2_U04_L08_GP_Q05::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L08_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: Unit 4 standards synthesis; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
Solve 4ˣ=30 using logarithms.
{
=A. x=log(30)/log(4)#Correct. Change of base isolates the exponent.
~B. x=log(4)/log(30)#This reverses the ratio.
~C. x=30/4#This treats the equation as linear.
~D. x=120#This multiplies base and output.
}

